-- 1: Detransition & regret
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

Estimates of how often people stop or reverse a transition range from well under 1% to around 30%, largely because studies measure different things. Clinic and registry studies counting formal reversals report the lowest figures: 0.6% of trans women and 0.3% of trans men restarted birth-sex hormones after gonad removal in the Amsterdam cohort (Wiepjes et al., 2018), 2.2% of 681 Swedish adults applied to reverse a legal gender change (Dhejne et al., 2014), and 21 of 2,467 Swedes later reversed a legal change (Clark et al., 2025). A meta-analysis of 27 surgical studies put regret at about 1% (Bustos et al., 2021). Prescription data diverge sharply: 98% of 720 Dutch adolescents still had a hormone prescription at a typical age of 19–20 (van der Loos et al., 2022), yet in US military health records an estimated 30% had stopped refilling within four years, with females on testosterone about 2.4 times as likely to stop (Roberts et al., 2022). A systematic review of 15 studies rated the whole evidence base very low quality (Feigerlova, 2025).

Surveys of detransitioners, who are mostly female (69% to 92%), describe varied reasons: becoming comfortable with their birth sex (60%), concern about medical complications (49%), and reinterpreting gender distress as rooted in trauma or mental illness (38%) (Littman, 2021). Many report unmet needs, with only 18% feeling adequately supported (Vandenbussche, 2022). By contrast, a survey limited to people who still identified as trans found 13% had ever detransitioned, mostly citing external pressures (Turban et al., 2021).

## Limits of the evidence

Regret appeared on average about 11 years after starting hormones in Amsterdam (Wiepjes et al., 2018) and about 8 years after legal change in Sweden (Dhejne et al., 2014), while many studies follow patients for 2–4 years. Loss to follow-up is substantial (36% in Amsterdam), and only 24% of surveyed detransitioners had told their clinicians (Littman, 2021), so clinic records likely undercount. Definitions range from a 90-day prescription gap to formal legal reversal. Most low-regret data come from adults treated under older, stricter protocols rather than today's largely adolescent, female patients (Cohn, 2023). Detransitioner surveys, in turn, recruit self-selected volunteers from online communities, cannot verify answers, and lack comparison groups. Neither body of evidence yet establishes a reliable detransition or regret rate.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'detransition';

-- 2: Detransitioner care & support
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

Studies of detransitioners' care needs agree closely on one point: people who stop or reverse transition report that health services are poorly prepared for them. In the largest survey, of 957 people in the US and Canada, 43% said providers never seemed knowledgeable about detransition and more than half sometimes or always avoided health care (Rackliff et al., 2025). Only 18% of 237 respondents from online detransition communities felt adequately supported (Vandenbussche, 2022), and only 24% of 100 detransitioners had told the clinicians who transitioned them (Littman, 2021), which suggests clinic records undercount this group. Interview studies describe people stopping hormones abruptly without medical supervision, clinicians unsure how to help, and reliance on online peer communities instead (MacKinnon et al., 2022; Lomax & Butler, 2025). Commonly reported needs include mental health care for conditions linked to the original distress, guidance on stopping hormones, help with reconstructive surgery, coping with regret, and contact with other detransitioners. Many respondents also reported feeling inadequately informed before treatment: 55% felt they had not been properly assessed (Littman, 2021), and 45% felt uninformed about health effects (Vandenbussche, 2022).

The populations behind these findings differ substantially. Surveys recruited through detransition networks are mostly female, and many respondents now identify with their birth sex and report regret, with about half reporting strong regret (Littman, 2021). Studies led by gender-affirming research teams used broad definitions that include people who still identify as trans, 42% of whom had restarted transition in one survey (Rackliff et al., 2025); in one interview study, 67% of 27 medically transitioned participants expressed no regret (MacKinnon et al., 2022). A proposed typology separates "core" detransition, a return to identifying with one's birth sex, from "non-core" stopping for external reasons (Expósito-Campos, 2021), and the apparent conflict largely reflects this difference in who was sampled.

## Limits of the evidence

The evidence consists of self-selected online surveys, small qualitative studies (6 to 28 interviewees), a clinician survey of 61 respondents, and commentaries. None has a comparison group of people who continued transition, medical histories are self-reported, and no study can estimate how common detransition is or test any model of care.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'detransitioner-care';

-- 3: Childhood desistance
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

Clinic follow-up studies of gender-dysphoric children consistently find that for most, gender dysphoria resolves by adolescence or adulthood without medical transition. The largest followed 139 boys seen in Toronto at about age 7 and reassessed them at about 21: 87.8% no longer had gender distress, including 86.4% of those who had met the full childhood diagnosis, while 12.2% still did (Singh et al., 2021). In Amsterdam, 63% of 127 children referred before age 12 no longer had gender dysphoria by their mid-teens; it persisted more often in girls (50%) than boys (29%), and persistence was associated with more intense childhood distress and with having socially transitioned (Steensma et al., 2013). Most desisters in these studies grew up same-sex or bisexually attracted. A compilation of 11 such studies put desistance at roughly 60% to 90% (Cantor, 2016). German insurance records point in a similar direction among older youth: only 36.4% of those diagnosed in 2017 still carried the diagnosis five years later (Bachmann et al., 2024).

Studies of socially transitioned children report the opposite pattern. Of 317 children who fully transitioned at ages 3 to 12, 94% identified as trans about five years later and about 60% had started blockers or hormones (Olson et al., 2022). Whether this reflects the children selected for transition or an effect of transition itself is the central unresolved question. Social transition has been argued to be an intervention that likely increases persistence (Zucker, 2020), while a study of 85 children found those who later transitioned already identified strongly with the other sex beforehand (Rae et al., 2019). A systematic review found no study following children forward with a suitable comparison group and concluded that neither benefit nor harm of social transition has been shown (Hall et al., 2024).

## Limits of the evidence

The desistance studies drew on single clinics and older, broader diagnostic criteria that included milder cases, often under therapy aimed at reducing cross-gender behavior, and in Amsterdam the 80 children who did not return were counted as desisters. The persistence studies used volunteer, mostly affluent and supportive families, with no formal diagnosis and short follow-up. Neither body of evidence reflects today's caseload, which is predominantly adolescent girls, and none can separate the effect of transition from pre-existing differences between children.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'childhood-desistance';

-- 4: Puberty blockers
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

Clinical cohorts show no measurable improvement in gender distress or mental health with puberty blockers. The first Dutch protocol study reported falls in behavior problems and depression (parent-reported clinical-range problems fell from 44% to 22%), but gender distress and body image did not change (de Vries et al., 2011). England's early-intervention study of 44 carefully selected 12–15-year-olds found no change in emotional distress, self-harm, quality of life or gender distress (Carmichael et al., 2021), and a US NIH-funded cohort of 94 youth found no measurable change over two years, with 18% moderately or severely depressed at baseline and 23% at two years (Olson-Kennedy et al., 2025). Formal reviews agree: a meta-analysis of 10 studies rated all mental health, gender distress and bone evidence very low certainty (Miroshnychenko et al., 2025); the York reviews for the Cass Review could draw no conclusions on gender distress or mental health (Cass Review, 2024); and an update of NICE's search found no new qualifying blocker study (Zepf et al., 2024). Blockers also rarely function as a neutral pause: 98% (Carmichael et al., 2021), 87% (Brik et al., 2020) and 100% (de Vries et al., 2011) went on to cross-sex hormones.

Physical outcomes show harm signals. Bone density falls behind peers during suppression; in one re-analysis about a third of patients had abnormally low hip density after two years (Biggs, 2021). At roughly age 28, testosterone-treated females had recovered, while estrogen-treated males retained low spine density, with 9–18% far below the normal range (van der Loos et al., 2023). None of 29 trans women who began blockers in early puberty had mature sperm at surgery (de Nie et al., 2022), and a small preprint reported shrunken, immature testicular tissue (Murugesh et al., 2024). Fertility preservation was rarely used: 2 of 73 teens despite near-universal counseling (Nahata et al., 2017).

## Limits of the evidence

No randomized trials exist, and nearly all studies lack an untreated comparison group, so changes cannot be separated from maturation, concurrent therapy or selection. Samples are small, attrition is heavy (37% missing depression data at two years in the US cohort; 14 of 44 remaining at three years in England), and most cohorts are pre-screened, clinic-run and followed only until hormones begin. Bone studies measure density, not fractures; fertility studies examine tissue, not fertility; long-term outcomes are essentially unstudied.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'puberty-blockers';

-- 5: Cross-sex hormones
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

Evidence that testosterone and estrogen improve mental health is consistently rated weak. A review of 20 studies found associations with better quality of life and less depression and anxiety, but graded every conclusion low strength and could reach none on suicide (Baker et al., 2021). A later review of 24 studies in people under 26 found no randomized trials, rated most mental-health findings very low certainty, and concluded that neither benefit nor harm could be ruled out (Miroshnychenko et al., 2025). Continuation figures vary sharply by setting: 98% of 720 Dutch adolescents who had first taken puberty blockers still held a prescription at a median age of about 19–20 (van der Loos et al., 2022), and a Boston clinic recorded 93% of 1,050 adolescents as continuing, although 210 of them had not been seen for over a year (Boskey et al., 2025). By contrast, about 30% of 952 US military-insured patients stopped within four years, testosterone users more often than estrogen users (Roberts et al., 2022). None measured regret or wellbeing.

Safety findings are more consistent. Trans women on estrogen had venous blood clots at 4.5 to 5.5 times expected population rates in an Amsterdam cohort (Nota et al., 2019), and a US cohort found the excess growing to about 17 extra clots per 1,000 compared with non-trans men after eight years (Getahun et al., 2018). Breast cancer in trans women was about 46 times the male rate but about a third of the female rate, based on 15 cases (de Blok et al., 2019), and the antiandrogen cyproterone acetate was linked to 7 to 19 times the risk of meningioma (Mikkelsen et al., 2022). In trans men, testosterone worsened cholesterol (Maraka et al., 2017), and the cumulative chance of exceeding the male red-cell limit reached 38% after ten years (Madsen et al., 2021).

## Limits of the evidence

Almost all of it is observational: single-clinic cohorts, registries adjusted for few variables, and self-selected surveys. Follow-up is often short, loss to follow-up is high, and people who stopped hormones are frequently excluded from safety analyses. An Australian registry reporting four to nine times general-population mortality could not separate the effect of hormones from underlying illness or other factors (Saxby et al., 2026).$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'cross-sex-hormones';

-- 6: Surgery
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

The evidence is most reliable for short-term complications. A systematic review of 39 mastectomy studies in people under 26 rated only complication outcomes as high certainty: no deaths, about 20 per 1,000 losing the nipple or graft, and about 50 per 1,000 with thick scarring (Miroshnychenko et al., 2024). In 209 adolescents at Kaiser Permanente, 7.3% of those followed for a year had a surgical complication and 10.9% needed revision, as the operation rate rose about 13-fold (Tang et al., 2022). For vaginoplasty, 26 mostly low-quality case series found stenosis the most common complication, and only one study measured quality of life (Horbach et al., 2015). Gender distress, mental health, suicide and quality of life were rated weak or very weak evidence, though one study suggested roughly halved non-suicidal self-harm after mastectomy (Miroshnychenko et al., 2024).

Long-term mental health data are sparse and contested. A Swedish registry study initially linked each year since surgery to 8% lower odds of mental health treatment, but a 2020 correction found no advantage compared with diagnosed people who did not have surgery (Bränström & Pachankis, 2020; Society for Evidence-Based Gender Medicine, 2020). An earlier Swedish cohort of 324 people found suicide deaths about 19 times higher than the general population, based on 10 deaths, but had no untreated comparison group (Dhejne et al., 2011). Reported regret is generally low: about 1% pooled across 27 studies (Bustos et al., 2021), 2.2% formal legal reversals in Sweden, typically about 8 years after transition (Dhejne et al., 2014), and 0.95% recorded in adolescent chart notes (Tang et al., 2022). Regret over fertility choices after reproductive-organ removal was much more common, at 39% (Johnson et al., 2024).

## Limits of the evidence

Almost no study compares operated patients with similar unoperated people, so benefit cannot be estimated. Regret estimates rest on short follow-up, heavy attrition (36% in the largest study pooled by Bustos et al., 2021), narrow definitions, and surgeon-run surveys with low response (Narayan et al., 2021; Bruce et al., 2023). Most data come from adults treated under older, stricter protocols. Breastfeeding loss after mastectomy is documented only in a single case report (Gribble et al., 2023). Many studies come from teams who perform these operations.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'surgery';

-- 7: Mental health & suicide
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

The most consistent finding is a heavy psychiatric burden, much of it predating any treatment. In Finland, 45.7% of gender-referred youth had needed specialist mental health care before referral, against 15.0% of matched peers (Ruuska et al., 2026); in English primary care, 52.7% had a record of anxiety, depression or self-harm (Jarvis et al., 2025). National registries agree that suicide and death rates are elevated: about 3.5 times the suicide rate and 7.7 times the rate of hospital-treated attempts in Denmark (Erlangsen et al., 2023), roughly twice the all-cause death rate in Sweden (Clark et al., 2026), and about 4 times the suicide rate in Taiwan, falling to 2.6 times against non-trans siblings (Hsu et al., 2025). Suicide nonetheless remains rare in absolute terms (0.03% of England's youth gender clinic patients; Biggs, 2022). In Finland, the excess suicide risk shrank and could be chance once psychiatric treatment history was accounted for (Ruuska et al., 2024).

Evidence that medical transition reduces this burden is weak. Systematic reviews rate mental health outcomes of puberty blockers and cross-sex hormones as very low certainty (Miroshnychenko et al., 2025) or low certainty, with no conclusion possible on suicide (Baker et al., 2021). Blocker cohorts in England and the US found no measurable change in distress or depression (Carmichael et al., 2021; Olson-Kennedy et al., 2025). A widely cited Swedish surgery study was corrected after a surgery versus no-surgery comparison of 1,018 people each showed no advantage (Bränström & Pachankis, 2020). In Finland, specialist psychiatric care among youth who transitioned rose from 9.8% to 60.7% (feminizing) and from 21.6% to 54.5% (masculinizing) after referral (Ruuska et al., 2026), and in Amsterdam suicides occurred at every stage, including 16 after completed surgery (Wiepjes et al., 2020). Links between chosen-name use or family acceptance and better mental health come from small cross-sectional surveys (Russell et al., 2018; Ryan et al., 2010), and a review found neither benefit nor harm of social transition established (Hall et al., 2024).

## Limits of the evidence

Registry studies are large but adjust for little beyond age and sex, rarely record hormone or surgery exposure, and often rest on few suicides (12 in Denmark; 10 in Dhejne et al., 2011). Authors' explanations, whether minority stress or pre-existing mental illness, go untested. Treatment studies lack untreated comparison groups and lose many participants, so causal effects in either direction remain unresolved.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'mental-health';

-- 8: Autism, trauma & co-occurring conditions
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

Across clinics in Finland, Australia, Italy and Taiwan, the finding that most consistently replicates is that people presenting with gender dysphoria carry a heavy burden of other psychiatric problems and adverse experiences. In Tampere, 75% of 47 adolescents had previously needed specialist mental health care for reasons other than gender, 26% were autistic, and 64% recalled no childhood gender distress (Kaltiala-Heino et al., 2015). In a Sydney service, 88% of 57 children had other diagnoses, they averaged 5.5 adverse childhood experiences versus 1.7 in community controls, and their attachment patterns were indistinguishable from those of children referred for other mental health problems (Kozlowska et al., 2021). Among 118 Italian adults, 29.6% met criteria for a dissociative disorder and 45.8% reported childhood trauma (Colizzi et al., 2015). Detransitioner case series and single-case reports describe similar histories, with mood disorders in 8 of 9 Finnish detransitioners and sexual abuse in 6 (Kettula et al., 2025).

Two national registry studies suggest co-occurring mental illness accounts for much of the elevated suicide risk. In Finland, gender-referred youth had about four times the suicide rate of peers, but after adjusting for specialist psychiatric care the difference fell below twofold and could be chance; the estimate rests on 7 suicides among 2,083 people (Ruuska et al., 2024). In Taiwan, suicide was about four times higher than in matched controls and 2.6 times higher than in siblings, and adjusting for depression attenuated the association most (Hsu et al., 2025). Both studies note that mental illness may lie on the pathway from gender distress rather than act as an independent cause.

## Limits of the evidence

Almost all studies here are cross-sectional or clinic-based snapshots, often without comparison groups, so they cannot establish whether trauma, autism or psychiatric illness precede, contribute to or follow gender dysphoria. Several papers from the same teams share patients and should not be counted as independent evidence. Case reports and small detransitioner series offer clinical detail but rely on retrospective, therapist-interpreted accounts, and several authors hold publicly stated positions on affirming care. The registry findings rest on few deaths and lack treatment data (Taiwan) or reliable treatment comparisons (Finland).$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'co-occurring-conditions';

-- 9: Rising diagnoses & social influence
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

The rise in diagnoses and the shift in who is presenting are the best-documented findings here, coming from large record-based studies in several countries. In English GP records, the share of under-19s with a recorded gender dysphoria diagnosis rose more than 50-fold between 2011 and 2021, to 8.3 per 10,000, and to 42 per 10,000 among 17- and 18-year-olds; by 2021 rates in girls were about twice those in boys (Jarvis et al., 2025). German insurance data on about 14 million young people showed an eight-fold rise in confirmed diagnoses from 2013 to 2022, highest in girls aged 15–19 (Bachmann et al., 2024). Earlier clinic data captured the start of the shift: in Toronto and Amsterdam, adolescent referrals flipped from a male to a female majority after 2006, while the sex ratio of other mental health referrals barely moved (Aitken et al., 2015). Co-occurring problems are consistently common: 52.7% in English GP records had anxiety, depression or self-harm recorded (Jarvis et al., 2025), 72.4% in Germany had another mental health diagnosis, and only 36.4% of those diagnosed in 2017 still carried the diagnosis five years later (Bachmann et al., 2024).

Why the rise occurred is far less settled. The "rapid-onset gender dysphoria" hypothesis originates in a survey of 256 parents recruited from websites skeptical of youth transition, who described prior mental health diagnoses and friend groups coming out together (Littman, 2018). A study of 173 adolescents under 16 at Canadian clinics found that those who recently realized their gender identity did not have more mental health problems or rely more on online or trans peers (Bauer et al., 2022), but critics argue it measured time since realization rather than onset relative to puberty, and only about 24 adolescents fell in the "recent" group. A narrative review judged the hypothesis unproven but worth serious study (Leonhardt et al., 2025).

## Limits of the evidence

Record-based studies count codes and referrals, not true prevalence, and cannot separate a real increase from greater awareness, reduced stigma, recoding or service changes; a dropped code is not necessarily desistance. No study directly tests peer or social media influence with prospective, population-level data, and the evidence on both sides of the social-influence question rests on small, self-selected or clinic-only samples.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'rising-diagnoses';

-- 10: Exploratory therapy
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$The literature on exploratory therapy consists almost entirely of clinical essays, single-case reports and one small case series, written mainly by the therapists who practice it. The only figures come from a review of 128 adolescents assessed at England's former national youth gender service: 12 (9.4%) whose distress began in adolescence stopped seeking medical treatment during an extended assessment averaging 6.6 sessions over 9.6 months (Churcher Clarke & Spiliadis, 2019). Nine of the 12 were girls and seven had an autism diagnosis; seven became comfortable with their birth sex, while five still identified as trans but no longer wanted hormones. Individual cases describe gender distress coming to be understood alongside grief, family conflict or abuse, such as a history of maternal abuse emerging only after 18 months of therapy (D'Angelo, 2025), or a 17-year-old who decided not to transition after five years of therapy (Lemma, 2018). Outcomes are not uniform: D'Angelo's patient still began estrogen at 18, and a 15-year-old who came to identify as gay and gender-fluid put a hormone referral on hold rather than ending it (Spiliadis, 2019).

Proponents describe the approach as open-ended and non-directive. Lemma explicitly rejects therapy aimed at changing identity, and Spiliadis frames it as a middle path between simple affirmation and conversion efforts. Critics argue the opposite: an essay posing 16 self-described leading questions compares the approach's language with past "ex-gay" programs and concludes it "may be unethical," though it presents no data and itself relies on affirming-care evidence later rated low quality (Ashley, 2023).

## Limits of the evidence

No study compares exploratory therapy with other approaches, uses validated measures of gender distress or mental health, or follows patients over the long term. The case series reports only the 12 cases with the outcome of interest, says nothing about the other 116, and tracked results for roughly 9 to 10 months. Case reports are chosen, anonymized and interpreted by the treating clinician, often an author with a stated position on youth gender medicine, and patients in long-term private psychoanalysis are an unusual group. The evidence shows that some young people's requests for medical treatment change during extended exploration, but not how often, for whom, or whether the therapy itself causes the change.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'exploratory-therapy';

-- 11: Guidelines & policy
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the reviews and appraisals show

The independent evidence reviews in this collection reach a consistent conclusion: the evidence that puberty blockers and cross-sex hormones improve gender distress, mental health or quality of life in minors is weak. The University of York reviews for England's Cass Review assessed 50 blocker studies and 53 hormone studies, each with only one rated high quality, and could draw no conclusions on blockers' effects on distress or mental health (Cass Review, 2024). An update of NICE's 2020 search found no new qualifying blocker study through 2023 (Zepf et al., 2024). The US government review, drawing on 17 earlier evidence reviews, rated evidence on mental health, quality of life and regret as very weak for every intervention (U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, 2025). A 2025 Utah review reached the opposite conclusion, but a critique argues it never graded overall certainty of evidence (Society for Evidence-Based Gender Medicine, 2026).

Appraisals of the guidelines themselves point the same way. Of 23 guidance documents rated with a standard appraisal instrument, only Sweden's and Finland's scored above 50% for rigor of development; WPATH's 2022 standards scored 35% and the Endocrine Society's 2017 guideline 42%, and most other guidance derived from those two (Taylor et al., 2024). A separate appraisal of six WPATH chapters scored rigor at 39–47% and applicability at 28–40%, with only two of eight raters recommending them unchanged (Zhang et al., 2026). The Endocrine Society graded the evidence behind every adolescent recommendation as low or very low yet issued some strong recommendations, and its commissioned reviews covered only bone and cardiometabolic safety (Hembree et al., 2017). WPATH stated that a systematic review of adolescent outcomes was "not possible" (Coleman et al., 2022).

## Limits of the evidence

Much of this literature appraises process and reporting rather than whether specific advice is right, and low guideline scores do not by themselves show harm. Several critiques were funded or written by organizations that campaign on the issue, and critics of the Cass Review also have stakes. Policy-effect evidence is thin: a self-published analysis of US state bans found suicide-rate changes among all 15–19-year-olds close to zero, but with one to three years of data and an outcome diluted across a population in which affected youth are a very small share (Greene & Kingsbury, 2026).$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'guidelines-and-policy';

-- 12: Medical ethics & consent
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

Most of the literature in this category is argument rather than data. The central ethical question is what justifies treatments for minors that may be irreversible. Gorin (2024) notes that the original Dutch protocol aimed to improve mental and physical health, and that systematic reviews in England, Sweden and Finland found weak evidence for those benefits. Gorin argues that a newer justification, based on patients' bodily goals and autonomy rather than health gains, conflicts with medicine's duty to avoid harm. On this view, autonomy means choosing among options that clinicians can justify. Commentaries by Jorgensen (2023) and Entwistle (2021) extend this concern to detransition. Jorgensen argues that rising youth detransition should be treated as iatrogenic harm, and that WPATH's 8th edition has no dedicated detransition chapter. Entwistle, a former clinician at England's youth gender service, argues that detransitioners' accounts point to decisions made on misleading information about benefit and need.

The empirical evidence on consent is thin but consistent with these concerns. In one US clinic, 72 of 73 adolescents (99%) had a documented fertility discussion before puberty blockers or hormones, yet only 2 (3%) attempted fertility preservation (Nahata et al., 2017). The study did not assess whether teens understood the risk of permanent infertility or how their wishes might change. In interviews with 28 Canadians who stopped or reversed transition, about one in three expressed regret or mixed feelings. Regret was often described together with feeling under-informed about risks and alternatives. Some participants felt that same-sex attraction, trauma, autism or mental illness had gone unexplored, and one had consented to testosterone while on a day pass from a psychiatric hold (MacKinnon et al., 2023). Most participants nonetheless still valued making their own decisions.

## Limits of the evidence

Three of the five items are opinion or philosophical pieces with no new data, and some drew published rebuttals. The two empirical studies are small. One relies on clinicians' notes and the other on self-selected, retrospective interviews, and neither measured understanding or decision-making capacity directly. No study here tested how consent processes affect outcomes, regret or later satisfaction, which remains the main gap.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'medical-ethics';

-- 13: Sex, biology & sport
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

This small, heterogeneous collection spans three questions: how sex is defined, whether transgender identity has a measurable basis in brain structure, and how sex differences bear on fairness in sport. On definition, one essay argues that in species with two gamete types there are exactly two sexes, male and female, defined by bodies organized to produce sperm or eggs, and that differences of sex development do not form a third sex (Wright, 2025). It presents an argument rather than new data, and a published critique contends that the definition presupposes its conclusion.

The "brain sex" evidence is thin. The best-known finding, that a small brain region (the BSTc) was female-typical in size in six trans women, came from postmortem tissue of people who had taken estrogen and anti-androgens for years, nearly all after castration (Zhou et al., 1995). A follow-up counted neurons in the same six brains rather than new cases (Kruijver et al., 2000), and the same group later reported that the sex difference in this region emerges only in adulthood. No independent team has replicated the finding. A 2026 reanalysis of published figures from a 2022 imaging study of 24 untreated trans women argues that their reported difference from men may not survive standard statistical corrections, although the difference from women does (Wright et al., 2026). Separately, early typology studies from one Toronto clinic reported sexual arousal to cross-dressing in 66–88% of male-to-female patients not exclusively attracted to men, against 15% of those attracted to men (Blanchard, 1985), and introduced the term autogynephilia (Blanchard, 1989); these ideas remain strongly disputed.

## Sport, and the limits of the evidence

On sport, the only entry is a 2022 sports medicine position statement arguing that male-level testosterone is the main driver of sex differences in muscle and performance, and favoring testosterone limits over the IOC's no-presumption-of-advantage framework (Pigozzi et al., 2022). It reviews no direct performance data on trans athletes after testosterone suppression. Across the category, the evidence consists of commentaries, very small postmortem samples confounded by hormone treatment, and single-clinic self-report studies without comparison groups. Several key authors have professional or advocacy ties on one side of the debate, and the category currently contains no empirical study of athletic performance.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'topic' AND slug = 'sex-and-biology';

-- 14: Systematic & evidence reviews
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the reviews show

These reviews converge on one finding: the evidence for psychological benefit from youth gender medicine is of low or very low certainty. Reviews from McMaster University found 10 puberty-blocker studies and 24 cross-sex hormone studies in people under 26, none randomized, with every mental health and gender-distress outcome rated very low certainty (Miroshnychenko et al., 2025). The University of York reviews for England's Cass Review could draw no conclusions on how blockers affect gender distress or mental health, and found only moderate-quality evidence that mental health may improve during hormone treatment (Cass Review, 2024). A German update of NICE's search found no new qualifying blocker study (Zepf et al., 2024), and a US government umbrella review of 17 earlier reviews rated outcomes for distress, mental health, quality of life and regret as very weak for every intervention (U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, 2025). The WPATH-commissioned review rated all its hormone findings low-strength while reporting improvements in quality of life, depression and anxiety (Baker et al., 2021). Social transition showed neither benefit nor harm (Hall et al., 2024). Only short-term mastectomy complications reached high certainty (Miroshnychenko et al., 2024).

Guideline appraisals point the same way. Of 23 youth guidelines, only Sweden's and Finland's were recommended by all three raters, with WPATH scoring 35% for rigor of development (Taylor et al., 2024); a separate appraisal of WPATH's Standards of Care 8 rated overall quality about 3.5 to 4 out of 7 (Zhang et al., 2026).

## Limits of the evidence

Reviews are only as strong as their underlying studies, which are overwhelmingly uncontrolled, small, short and affected by loss to follow-up. Regret and detransition reviews illustrate this: a pooled 1% surgical regret figure rests on follow-up often of one to two years and 36% attrition in the largest study (Bustos et al., 2021), while headline detransition ranges under 10% omit an included military study in which about 30% stopped hormones within four years (Feigerlova, 2025). Older Endocrine Society-commissioned reviews found too few cardiovascular events or fractures to assess risk (Maraka et al., 2017; Singh-Ospina et al., 2017). Funding and authorship are contested on both sides, from SEGM-funded McMaster reviews to WPATH's control over publication, and several searches end in 2021–2023. Low-certainty evidence signals uncertainty, not proof of no effect.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'method' AND slug = 'systematic-reviews';

-- 15: Cohort & registry studies
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

Cohort and registry studies supply most of this field's hard numbers on diagnoses, treatment continuation, side effects and deaths. Population records show a steep rise in diagnoses, about 50-fold in English GP records from 2011 to 2021 (Jarvis et al., 2025). National mortality studies agree that death and suicide rates are raised. All-cause mortality was roughly double that of matched controls in Sweden (Clark et al., 2026), and suicide was 3.5 times higher in Denmark (Erlangsen et al., 2023). Where psychiatric history was examined, it explained much of the gap. In Finland, the excess suicide risk became compatible with chance once specialist psychiatric care was accounted for (Ruuska et al., 2024). In Taiwan, adjusting for depression shrank the suicide link the most (Hsu et al., 2025). Clinic cohorts also report physical risks, such as 4.5 to 5.5 times the expected rate of venous blood clots in trans women (Nota et al., 2019).

Uncontrolled cohorts of youth on puberty blockers found no measurable change in gender distress (de Vries et al., 2011; Carmichael et al., 2021) or in depression over two years (Olson-Kennedy et al., 2025), and nearly all went on to cross-sex hormones. Continuation figures conflict. In Amsterdam, 98% of youth still had a hormone prescription at around age 20 (van der Loos et al., 2022), while about 30% stopped within four years in US military records (Roberts et al., 2022).

## Limits of the evidence

Most of these studies lack an untreated comparison group. Registry studies typically match only on age and sex, so they show that risks are raised but not why. Key estimates often rest on a handful of events, such as 12 suicides in Denmark. Regret is measured through narrow proxies over short follow-up. In Sweden, 21 of 2,467 people reversed a legal gender change (Clark et al., 2025). In Amsterdam, regret took about 11 years on average to appear, and 36% of patients stopped attending the clinic (Wiepjes et al., 2018). Many cohorts are clinics reporting on their own care. One Norwegian cohort was retracted on ethics grounds (Nyquist et al., 2025), and a widely cited surgery study was corrected after a reanalysis found no advantage of surgery (Bränström & Pachankis, 2020).$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'method' AND slug = 'cohort-studies';

-- 16: Surveys
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What surveys have contributed

Surveys are the main first-hand source on detransition, which clinic records largely miss: only 24% of 100 detransitioners had told their transitioning clinicians (Littman, 2021), and 27.1% in a later sample (Littman et al., 2024). Detransitioner respondents are mostly born female, and themes recur: many came to see their gender distress as linked to trauma or other mental health problems (38% in Littman, 2021; 70% in Vandenbussche, 2022), many felt inadequately assessed or informed about risks, and few felt supported afterward: only 18% did (Vandenbussche, 2022). A North American survey of 957 people found 43% said providers never seemed knowledgeable about detransition (Rackliff et al., 2025). Findings depend heavily on who is sampled. Among 2,242 currently trans-identified respondents who had ever detransitioned, 83% cited at least one external pressure and 16% an internal reason (Turban et al., 2021), but that survey excluded anyone who no longer identified as trans. Regret surveys are similarly divided: surgeons recalled 62 regret cases among an estimated 18,125 to 27,325 patients (Narayan et al., 2021), and a single-hospital mastectomy survey reported a typical regret score of zero (Bruce et al., 2023), whereas 39% of patients reported some regret about fertility choices after reproductive-organ removal (Johnson et al., 2024). Surveys have also documented pelvic and genital pain in testosterone users (Zwickl et al., 2023; Tordoff et al., 2023) and generated disputed hypotheses, from Blanchard's typology (Blanchard, 1985; Blanchard, 1989) to "rapid-onset gender dysphoria" (Littman, 2018).

## Strengths and limits

Surveys capture reasons, needs and perceptions invisible to clinics, but their weaknesses are consistent. Almost all samples are self-selected, recruited through online communities, advocacy groups or partisan websites, so they cannot estimate how common any experience is. Most are one-time, retrospective and unverified against records, often use untested questionnaires and lack comparison groups, so they show associations rather than causes. Examples include chosen-name use and depression in 74 youth (Russell et al., 2018) and recalled family acceptance among 245 LGBT adults, only about 22 of them trans (Ryan et al., 2010). Response bias can run in either direction: 41% of mastectomy patients did not reply and 13 high-regret answers were excluded (Bruce et al., 2023), while detransitioner surveys may over-represent people with negative experiences. Definitions of detransition vary widely, and researchers' positions on either side of the debate shape question design and interpretation.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'method' AND slug = 'surveys';

-- 17: Interviews & qualitative research
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What the studies show

These interview studies of people who stopped or reversed a gender transition capture what surveys and registries cannot: reasons, shifting identities and experiences of care. Across three samples, several themes recur. Body discomfort could return after stopping, or turn into distress about permanent changes such as a deeper voice (Pullen Sansfaçon et al., 2023). They describe same-sex attraction, sometimes with shame about being gay, trauma, autism or mental illness as factors that fed their distress but were not explored by clinicians (Pullen Sansfaçon et al., 2023; MacKinnon et al., 2023). They report feeling under-informed about risks before treatment (Lomax & Butler, 2025; MacKinnon et al., 2023). Many stopped hormones without medical supervision and turned to online detransition communities for support (MacKinnon et al., 2022; Lomax & Butler, 2025). Identities after stopping were varied. In the Canadian sample, 60% moved from a binary trans identity to a nonbinary one (MacKinnon et al., 2023).

Accounts of regret differ by sample. In the Canadian study, 6 of 27 participants who medically transitioned (22%) were judged to regret it and 3 (11%) had mixed feelings, while 18 (67%) expressed no regret (MacKinnon et al., 2022). That sample was recruited broadly, and 75% still identified as trans. The UK study was smaller and recruited through detransition networks. Its six women, four of whom had had double mastectomies, said hormones and surgery had not resolved their distress or changed their sex. Some also came to see narrow ideas of womanhood as having shaped their original decision (Lomax & Butler, 2025).

## Limits of the evidence

The evidence base is very small: three independent samples totaling 54 people, because the two Canadian papers re-analyze the same 28 interviews. All samples were self-selected, mostly through social media and detransition or trans networks, and were predominantly born female. Accounts are retrospective, given at a single interview and not checked against medical records. "Regret" was coded by researchers from conversation rather than measured with a validated instrument, so none of these figures are rates. Several teams acknowledge that their own positions may have shaped the accounts and themes. These studies generate hypotheses and describe unmet needs. They cannot show how common any experience is.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'method' AND slug = 'qualitative-research';

-- 18: Case reports
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$Case reports in this collection are detailed accounts of single patients or small clinical series, most of them describing people who detransitioned or whose requests for medical treatment changed. Their main contribution is depth: they document pathways, harms and clinical encounters that larger datasets rarely capture. The largest is a Finnish series of all nine adults who returned to a Helsinki gender clinic to detransition in 2018–2019; regret took seven years on average to appear, eight had undergone breast surgery, and mood disorders (89%), anxiety disorders (78%) and borderline personality disorder (56%) were common (Kettula et al., 2025). A UK series found 12 of 128 adolescents (9.4%) stopped seeking medical treatment during extended assessment, most of them girls and seven with an autism diagnosis (Churcher Clarke & Spiliadis, 2019). Single cases describe harms not usually measured, such as grief at being unable to breastfeed after mastectomy at 20, with no recalled pre-surgical discussion of it (Gribble et al., 2023), or testosterone prescribed at 18 after a 30-minute appointment, followed by two psychiatric hospitalizations (Marchiano, 2021).

A recurring theme is that gender distress came to be understood, in hindsight or in therapy, alongside trauma, grief, family conflict or difficulty growing up female (D'Angelo, 2020; D'Angelo, 2025; Kettula et al., 2025). These reports have also prompted practical recommendations, including breastfeeding counseling before mastectomy and easier routes for detransitioners to return to clinics.

## Limits of the evidence

The method cannot establish how common any of these experiences are, nor what caused them. Samples are tiny, there are no comparison groups, and percentages in the Finnish series rest on counts below ten, with some figures in its discussion not matching its own tables. Accounts are often reconstructed after regret, from memory or clinical notes, and interpreted by the treating clinician without validated measures or independent review. Details are frequently altered for anonymity, and several authors hold stated positions on gender medicine. Case reports are best read as hypothesis-generating signals that point to questions for larger, prospective studies.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'method' AND slug = 'case-reports';

-- 19: Commentary & analysis
UPDATE study_categories SET summary = $ov$## What commentary contributes

Much of the most-cited material in this field is argument rather than new data. Its main contribution has been scrutiny of how evidence is graded and used. The Endocrine Society's 2017 guideline rated the evidence behind every adolescent recommendation as low or very low yet still made some strong recommendations (Hembree et al., 2017), a gap a BMJ investigation examined with guideline-methods experts (Block, 2023). WPATH's eighth Standards of Care state that a systematic review for adolescents was "not possible" and grade no evidence (Coleman et al., 2022). Re-analyses have also narrowed headline claims: a journal correction found "no advantage of surgery" when 1,018 surgical patients in a Swedish registry study were compared with 1,018 similar patients (Society for Evidence-Based Gender Medicine, 2020), and individual scans from 24 English adolescents on puberty blockers showed about a third with abnormally low hip bone density after two years (Biggs, 2021). A recurring argument is that detransition and regret rates are unknown rather than low: published times to regret run from about 3 to over 10 years, and no study in a 2021 surgery-regret review followed at least 85% of patients for eight years (Cohn, 2023), while only about a quarter of surveyed detransitioners told their clinicians (Irwig, 2022).

Commentary has also supplied frameworks: a distinction between "core" and "non-core" detransition (Expósito-Campos, 2021), calls for follow-up of at least 5 to 10 years (MacKinnon et al., 2023), and an exploratory therapy model (Spiliadis, 2019). Pieces cut in different directions: one bioethicist argues serious treatments for minors need evidence of health benefit, not patient wishes alone (Gorin, 2024), while another essay likens exploratory therapy to conversion practices and cites a high detransition estimate of about 3% (Ashley, 2023).

## Limits of the evidence

By design these papers add no new data, rarely use a systematic search, and select sources to fit an argument. Many authors have declared stakes, including funding from advocacy groups, think-tank affiliations, clinic roles and campaigning on either side, and some pieces received light review, one accepted nine days after submission (Wright et al., 2026). Many lean on the same few primary sources, such as one military pharmacy dataset and self-selected detransitioner surveys. Their claims are hypotheses and critiques for primary research to test.$ov$, summary_translation = NULL, updated_at = now() WHERE kind = 'method' AND slug = 'commentary';
